answer to question two:

some parameters MITS did not specified. instead authors of MITS said  they used parameters of two other articles (article one, article two) for MITS simulation. I also added my opinion option. in this option I tried to address most likely and another possibility of values for parameters. this option is arbitrary and can be used only when others (article one, article two) values fails.

article two parameters is from the paper with doi: 10.1109/TVT.2022.3196544

article one parameters is from the paper with doi: 10.1109/TVT.2018.2881191

but before I told you the parameters of other articles, there are some reminder about main article I have to mention.

* article uses OFDMA for transmission

* article calculate uplink transmittion (task offloading) and download transmission (send back output of processed tasks)

* uplink and down link channels are the same.

*if there is a parameter value that main article specified, that value is only possible value. thus the parameters in main article are important.

* in all options (articleone, article two, my optinon), vehicles battery are large enough to process.

* in all articles, the number of sub-bands for OFDMA channels set equal to the number of users per cell.

*in the main article (mits) authors used 0.5 for both lambdas in objective function.

article one:
for base station spacing we have options:
1. 237m
2. 209m
3. 112m

channel gain is :
generated using a distance-dependent path-loss model given as 140.7+36.7 * log_{10} ^ d_{km}, d is the Euclidean distance
and log-normal shadowing is 8dB

users transmission power (tx) is 20dBm

user bandwidth is: 20 MHz

noise is -100dBm

chip energy coeffient (k) is 5 * 10^-27

output file data size is: 420 KB

the users are dropped in random locations, with uniform distribution, within the coverage area of the network at the beginning
of the simulations 

number of sub-bands in OFDMA sets to number of users per cells.

second article:
range of RSUs: 250m

pathloss is 128.9+37.6 * (log_{10} ^{d_{km}}, d is Euclidean distance in kilimeter and shadowing effect is log-normal with 8dB and fast fading is Rayleigh fading distribution

tx power of user is: 23 dBm

noise is: -114 dBm

chip energy coeffient (k) is: 10^-25

number of sub-bands in OFDMA sets to number of users per cells.

my opinion:
user transmission rate is: 8 mbps

range of RSUs is 100m or 200m or 250m

noise can be: 1.5 * 10^-8 W

user tx power: 2 +0.2 or 2-0.2 W

channel gain: 144 + 14.4 or 144 - 14.4 dB

discount factor for distance can be: 0.9 , 2

time slot could be: 20sec, 10sec or 5sec

note that in any of above options (article one, article two, my option) if there was a parameters value that did not mentioned in any other options you should treat that value as your only possible value and can be used in any other options.

for the sake of understanding first use article one and after that article two parameters separately. then test each article individually. then you can use combinations of parameters from article one and article two. if it did not worked you can then use parameters value in my opinion.

after failing at all of the values you can use common parameters of your choose but after testing the parameters i gave to you. if you used any other parameters please write in your report file.
