answer to question three:

In addition at the beginning, the users are dropped in random locations, with uniform distribution, within the coverage area of the network.

the article (MITS) said all RSUs are randomly deployed and all RSUs are connected to the Base station. but in the file tmccode.py there is hard coded RSUs ID. please consider this in your code. I think it is more likely authors did not used hard coded RSUs. instead they really deployed RSUs randomly or they process traces of vehicles and deployed RSUs in their possible route.

the coverage area of RSUs do not overlap. so user can only connect to one RSU in each time slot.

also, I provide you with additional information:

file tmccode.rar i gave to you provides the simulation area setup (i.e., distribution of MECs) and the mobility prediction using Markov chain in the file tmccode.cpp witch is main code file.

please ignore files requests.txt, result.txt and irreverent other files or folders. you job for file tmccode.rar is only understand tmccode.py file. the file tmccode.cpp is same as tmccode.py but in c++ language.

in main article (mits) users are vehicles witch authors used "cabspottingdata" file. but in tmccoded.rar users considered PDA and pedestrians. Ap_location.csv and wtd.csv and request.txt file are only for your understanding of the main code tmccode.py.  in real implementation, after undrestanding the tmccode.py file, use "cabspottingdata" file as your source file for users mobility. 

please note that file tmccode.rar only provide information about distribution of MECs, RSUs and the users mobility prediction using Markov chain algorithm. it just for your understanding about simulation environment. for information about users (vehicles) please use ./cabspottingdata/ instead of wtd.csv file. for example in tmccode.py file authors used markov chain to predict users mobility from file wtd.csv but instead of wtd.csv you use files in ./cabspottingdata/ to predict. each txt file in ./cabspottingdata/ represent one user (vehicle). if there are any questions ask.

also, in section VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION in article, and subsection E. Results and Discussion authors considered different scenarios. in items impact of number of tasks , Impact of Number of Vehicles and Impact of Number of RSUs authors describe impact of number of tasks, number of vehicles and RSUs by varying them from minimum number to maximum number. so if you read this items you will understand for each item (we call scenarios) what you should do. please write your job in each scenario in your documentation. please only consider these scenarios
